## A Statistically Sour Summer

This year has already seen 759 recalls, with a shocking 206 of them classified as Class I--the most serious category implicating health hazards. July started on a bitter note with multiple recalls, including salmonella and botulism risks. Notably, the infamous repeat offenders continue to mar the industry. 

## July's Recall Rundown

In the first week of July, Utz Quality Foods issued recalls for three different potato chip products due to potential salmonella contamination, demonstrating a pressing need for better quality control in snack foods. Another standout recall involves Prime Food Processing LLC's Dried Herring Fish, flagged for improper evisceration--a process that can lead to botulism contamination.

Spring & Mulberry Inc. once again failed to deliver safe products, recalling their Mango Chili Date-Sweetened Chocolate and Mulberry Fennel Date-Sweetened Chocolate due to salmonella risk. This marks their 11th recall this year, making them one of the most egregious offenders.

## The Repeat Offender Epidemic

93 brands have faced repeat recalls this year alone. Among them, Wellnov Supplements LLC and Diva Fam Inc. top the list, each with a concerning 14 recalls. Spring & Mulberry Inc. and Moonlight Packing Corporation round out the "worst offenders" list with double-digit Class I recalls. This trend exposes systemic failures in maintaining safety standards.

## July's Class Breakdown

Class I recalls dominated the first week of July, with the potential for salmonella and botulism being the primary culprits. Class II recalls also made a strong showing, involving allergen labeling errors and inadequate pasteurization of lassi products.

## What Lies Ahead

With 45 recalls already reported in July, the trend suggests another high month for recalls reminiscent of June's record-breaking 213 incidents. Consumers are advised to inspect their purchases carefully and stay informed about the latest recalls.

Stay sharp, stay safe.
